/goal check now honours the /cd deny list, and a new repo-wide test fails if any other file checks a path against the startup fence without either honouring that list or being registered with a reason. Five files still skip the list: commands_map.rs, commands_project.rs, commands_rename.rs, context.rs and skill_fence.rs. Build, test, clippy and fmt all pass. The commit is b3abc3ad. It's local only; the session push will send it.

Where the deny list lives. The list stays outside DirectoryRestrictions::check_path, so that function stays pure and its existing tests don't change. I wrote this decision into ARCHITECTURE.md along with the option I turned down: putting the list inside the check would protect every caller automatically, but all of its tests would then depend on global state. The cost of my choice is that a path check nobody updates still ignores the list after /cd, and the new test is what pays for that.

The test (no_check_path_reader_skips_the_cd_deny_list_unregistered, in src/cd_config_note.rs):
• It scans the non-test code in every src/**/*.rs file for .check_path( calls.
• A call passes if the function around it also calls one of the /cd deny checks. Otherwise its file has to be listed in UNWIRED_CHECK_PATH_READERS with a reason naming #869.
• A listed file that no longer has an unwired call also fails the test. So the list has to shrink by exactly the number of files fixed, and its length is the count of what's left.
• It only proves the /cd check is called in the same function, not that it runs. A plain std::fs read with no check_path call at all is on no list.

The real count was 6, not 5. The #869 comment's list missed src/skill_fence.rs, so I built the list from what the code actually contains. I noted the difference in ARCHITECTURE.md (the old sentence is marked superseded, not deleted) and in the #869 comment.

Why `/goal check`: it's the one unwired reader that clearly runs after a /cd and reads a file in the new directory. It looks for the goal file relative to . at the moment it runs, so after /cd it reads the new directory's .yoyo/goal.md.
• The startup check runs first and gives exactly the same result and wording as before.
• The /cd check comes next, and its refusal ends with (a [directories] deny entry applied by /cd), the same suffix @path uses.
• New tests compare the full refusal, check that a near-miss sibling directory still loads, and check that an empty /cd list gives the old result byte for byte across four startup setups.
• The list went from 6 files to 5.

Positive controls, run one at a time and both restored:
• Adding a dummy check_path call to banner.rs made the new test fail and name that file.
• Disabling the new goal check failed the /cd refusal test and the new test, while the byte-identical test stayed green.

I also updated docs/src/configuration/permissions.md: /goal check is now on the list of places that honour the deny list, and skill loading is added to the readers that don't. I commented on #869 with the decision, the test, the reader I updated and the count by file, and left the issue open.

